---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Bosphorus uptown: the ferry to Yoros · HeyaGuide"
  description="The classic Bosphorus-line ferry from Eminönü to Anadolu Kavağı, with stops at the Bosphorus palaces and ending at a Byzantine fortress. The best urban ferry ride in the world."
>
  <article class="trip">
    <div class="trip-hero">
      <img src="/trips/bosphorus-hero.jpg" alt="Şehir Hatları Bosphorus ferry" />
    </div>

    <header class="trip-head container">
      <p class="trip-eyebrow">Day trip from Heya</p>
      <h1>Bosphorus uptown: the ferry to Yoros</h1>
      <p class="trip-lede">
        The single best urban ferry ride in the world runs up the
        Bosphorus from Eminönü to Anadolu Kavağı at the Black Sea
        mouth. It threads past the imperial palaces, under both
        Bosphorus bridges, and ends at a 14th-century fortress where two
        seas meet. Half a day, an Istanbulkart, and the river does the
        rest.
      </p>
      <div class="trip-meta">
        <div class="trip-meta-item">
          <span class="trip-meta-label">From Heya</span>
          <span class="trip-meta-value">~3 h 30 min total (including ferry)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trip-meta-item">
          <span class="trip-meta-label">Ferry ride</span>
          <span class="trip-meta-value">~2 h 15 min each way</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trip-meta-item">
          <span class="trip-meta-label">Season</span>
          <span class="trip-meta-value">Apr–Oct daily · Nov–Mar weekends only</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="trip-body container">
      <p class="trip-lede-2">
        The Bosphorus line is the older, more local version of the famous
        Bosphorus cruise boats that charge tourists ₺200 for an hour. The
        Şehir Hatları municipal ferry does the full 35 km of the strait
        for the price of an Istanbulkart ride, and you can hop on with
        your groceries. The ride is the same one the Ottomans used
        centuries ago, and the views are unchanged.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting to Eminönü from Heya</h2>
      <p>
        Take the F1 funicular from Taksim down to Kabataş, then the T1
        tram one stop to Karaköy, then walk 10 minutes across the
        Galata Bridge to the Eminönü ferry terminal. The full route is
        about 30 minutes from Heya.
      </p>
      <p>
        Buy or top up your Istanbulkart before you reach the terminal — the
        Istanbulkart machines are at every metro and tram stop, and the
        card works on every ferry, tram, and bus in the city.
      </p>

      <h2>On the ferry: the lower Bosphorus</h2>
      <p>
        The Bosphorus line departs Eminönü once or twice a day (1–2 sailings
        each direction). In summer the morning ferry leaves around 10:00;
        in winter only on weekends. Get there 20 minutes before to grab a
        good seat on the upper deck — the right side gives the best views
        going up, the left side coming back.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first hour is the dense part of the strait. The ferry
        threads past:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Topkapı Palace and Hagia Sophia</strong> skyline behind you on the European side</li>
        <li><strong>Dolmabahçe Palace</strong> on the European side — the long baroque façade right on the water</li>
        <li><strong>Çırağan Palace</strong> (now a hotel) just past it</li>
        <li><strong>Beşiktaş ferry terminal</strong> on the European side — you can join the ferry here if you prefer</li>
        <li><strong>Ortaköy Mosque</strong> on the European side, with its single minaret on the water</li>
        <li><strong>First Bosphorus Bridge</strong> — the boat passes under the road deck; bring a long lens if you have one</li>
        <li><strong>Çırağan Park</strong> and the Yıldız Park forest on the European side</li>
        <li><strong>Beylerbeyi Palace</strong> on the Asian side — a smaller white Ottoman summer palace</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The middle Bosphorus: palaces and villages</h2>
      <p>
        After the first bridge, the strait gets narrower and the
        waterfront changes from imperial to residential — old yalıs
        (waterfront mansions), boatyards, fishing villages. The ferry
        calls at <strong>Beşiktaş</strong> on the European side, then
        continues up the strait. Around the 90-minute mark you'll pass:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Rumeli Hisarı</strong> on the European side — the great fortress Mehmed II built in 1452, a year before the conquest. The ferry passes close enough to see the towers.</li>
        <li><strong>Fatih Sultan Mehmet Bridge</strong> (the second bridge) — a more modern suspension bridge from 1988</li>
        <li><strong>Yoros Castle (Anadolu Hisarı)</strong> on the Asian side — the 14th-century fortress that started it all</li>
        <li><strong>Küçüksu Palace</strong> on the Asian side — a small rococo palace right on the water</li>
        <li><strong>Kanlıca</strong> on the Asian side — a quiet village famous for its yogurt with powdered sugar (a real thing, and worth trying if the ferry stops long enough)</li>
        <li><strong>Yeniköy and Büyükdere</strong> on the European side — old fishing villages now absorbed into the city, with surviving wooden yalıs on the waterfront</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Final stop: Anadolu Kavağı and Yoros Castle</h2>
      <p>
        About 2 h 15 min after departure, the ferry docks at
        <strong>Anadolu Kavağı</strong>, a small village at the
        Black-Sea mouth of the strait. The village is small and the main
        reason to come is the walk (or dolmuş ride) up to
        <a href="/places/yoros-castle">Yoros Castle</a> on the hill
        above.
      </p>
      <p>
        From the pier it's a 20-minute walk uphill to the castle
        (follow the signs, the path is paved and not steep). The fortress
        itself is a weathered ruin — broken stone walls, a roofless inner
        tower, and a few remaining gun positions — but the view from
        the top is the reason to make the climb: the Bosphorus stretching
        south, the Black Sea opening north, and the green Asian shore
        falling away on both sides.
      </p>
      <p>
        For lunch back in the village, there are three or four waterfront
        fish restaurants with terraces over the water. The grilled fish
        is fresh; the mezes are simple; the price is fair.
      </p>

      <h2>The ferry back</h2>
      <p>
        The return ferry from Anadolu Kavağı leaves in the mid-afternoon
        (around 15:00 in summer). Confirm the exact time when you arrive
        in the morning, and plan to be at the pier 15 minutes before.
        The return trip takes a similar 2 hours and arrives back at
        Eminönü around 5 p.m. You'll be back at Heya by 6 p.m. with
        the rest of the evening free.
      </p>

      <h2>Practicalities</h2>
      <ul class="trip-practical">
        <li><strong>Istanbulkart:</strong> load at least ₺100 at the metro before you leave Heya — the ferry is a few trips' worth.</li>
        <li><strong>What to bring:</strong> sunscreen, water, snacks (the ferry has a small tea-and-sandwich kiosk but it's basic), a light jacket if it's breezy. No food or drinks of your own are technically allowed on the ferry but a water bottle is fine.</li>
        <li><strong>Seating:</strong> the upper rear deck has the best views and is usually less crowded than the bow. Get there early.</li>
        <li><strong>Season:</strong> this trip only really works in April through October. The winter schedule has only weekend sailings and the castle walk can be muddy.</li>
        <li><strong>If it's cancelled:</strong> ferries are sometimes cancelled for weather. Check the Şehir Hatları schedule the day before at sehirhatlari.istanbul.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related places</h2>
      <ul class="trip-related">
        <li><a href="/places/yoros-castle">Yoros Castle</a> — the fortress at the Black Sea mouth</li>
        <li><a href="/places/kucuksu-palace">Küçüksu Palace</a> — the small rococo palace the ferry passes</li>
        <li><a href="/places/beylerbeyi-palace">Beylerbeyi Palace</a> — the larger white summer palace</li>
        <li><a href="/ferries/">All ferries & schedules →</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .trip {
    background: var(--color-bg-elevated);
  }

  .trip-hero {
    aspect-ratio: 21/9;
    max-height: 420px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--color-bg-muted);
  }

  .trip-hero img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .trip-head {
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--container-pad) var(--space-6);
    text-align: center;
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .trip-eyebrow {
    font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wider);
    color: var(--color-brand);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .trip-head h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
  }

  .trip-lede {
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
  }

  .trip-meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-4) var(--space-6);
    justify-content: center;
    padding: var(--space-4) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .trip-meta-item {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 2px;
  }

  .trip-meta-label {
    font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wider);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .trip-meta-value {
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .trip-body {
    max-width: 720px;
    padding: var(--space-7) var(--container-pad) var(--space-9);
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .trip-lede-2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
  }

  .trip-body h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-xl);
    margin: var(--space-7) 0 var(--space-3);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .trip-body p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
  }

  .trip-body ul {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
  }

  .trip-body li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .trip-body a {
    color: var(--color-brand);
  }

  .trip-practical li {
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .trip-practical strong,
  .trip-related strong {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .trip-related {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  .trip-related li {
    padding: var(--space-2) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .trip-related li:first-child,
  .trip-related li:nth-child(2) {
    border-top: none;
  }

  .trip-related a {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .trip-hero {
      aspect-ratio: 4/3;
      max-height: 280px;
    }
    .trip-related {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
